<h2 class="headline">About This Project</h2>
<p class="byline">An independent civic-data project for {{ site.city }}</p>

<p class="lede">
  {{ site.name }} exists to make it easier for residents to see how their city government spends money —
  without waiting for anyone to ask, and without needing to open a 50-page PDF to find the answer.
</p>

<p>
  It's inspired by a small wave of independent municipal-finance transparency sites built by residents in
  Massachusetts towns — most directly <a href="https://marbleheaddata.org/">Marblehead Data</a>, a
  resident-built, open-source site that turns Marblehead's own budget, spending, and debt documents into
  plain-language explanations rather than PDFs only a finance professional could parse. It's in the same
  spirit as the state's own <a href="https://www.macomptroller.org/cthru/">CTHRU</a> checkbook for state
  government and the <a href="https://www.mass.gov/municipal-databank-data-analytics">Division of Local
  Services' municipal databank</a> for statewide local-government trends. Where Fitchburg doesn't yet have
  its own equivalent, this project tries to fill the gap using only what the city already publishes.</p>

